@page
@model CSharpMasteryHub.Pages.AspNetCoreRestApis.SecuringApisModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Securing Your API";
}

<div class="container">
    <div class="row">
        <div class="col-lg-9">
            <div class="lesson-content">
                <h1 class="display-4 mb-3">Securing Your API</h1>
                <div class="d-flex gap-3 mb-4">
                    <span class="badge bg-danger">Advanced</span>
                    <span class="badge bg-info text-dark">30 min read</span>
                    <span class="badge bg-primary">Lesson 10 of 10</span>
                </div>

                <div class="alert alert-danger">
                    <i class="fas fa-shield-alt me-2"></i> <strong>The VIP Club Story:</strong> Your API is a VIP club. You don't want just anyone walking in! You need a bouncer at the door. In ASP.NET Core, the bouncer is the <code>[Authorize]</code> attribute, and the VIP pass is the user's <strong>Token</strong>.
                </div>

                <h2>Connecting to C#</h2>
                <p>If you have a C# method that deletes a user, you want to ensure only an Admin can call it. You don't write <code>if (user.IsAdmin)</code> inside the method. Instead, you let the framework handle security before the method is even executed.</p>

                <h2>Authentication vs Authorization</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Authentication (AuthN):</strong> "Who are you?" (Logging in).</li>
                    <li><strong>Authorization (AuthZ):</strong> "Are you allowed to do this?" (Checking permissions).</li>
                </ul>

                <h2>Example 1: The Bouncer ([Authorize])</h2>
                <p>By adding the <code>[Authorize]</code> attribute to your Controller, you block all unauthenticated users from accessing it.</p>
<pre><code>[Authorize] // <--- The Bouncer
[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class SecretController : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpGet("admin-data")]
    public IActionResult GetSecretData()
    {
        // Only logged-in users can see this
        return Ok("This is top secret data!");
    }
}</code></pre>

                <h2>Example 2: API Keys (A simple approach)</h2>
                <p>Sometimes you just want to give a password to another developer so their app can talk to yours. You check for an "X-API-KEY" in the request headers.</p>
<pre><code>[HttpGet]
public IActionResult GetData([FromHeader(Name = "X-API-KEY")] string apiKey)
{
    if (apiKey != "my-super-secret-key-123")
    {
        return Unauthorized("Invalid API Key!"); // Returns 401
    }

    return Ok("Welcome to the API!");
}</code></pre>

                <div class="card bg-info bg-opacity-10 mb-4 mt-4">
